  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/241d4b1d29efddc5a9feb13b835d0caab6af354d3f62387dc92d14ff81942a6d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{RP}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{RP}=$   \n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $R$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RP}=\\overset{\\to }{RT}+\\overset{\\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/b4ebdca78235f8f82564db1821320c7bb886447cb012813173e9c50558dbc9bb.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/540f94306cc497d87dc1f15d61ea929e042944f7c6f711a2bcf6b1f944c48228.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RP} & =\\overset{\\to }{RT}+\\overset{\\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP} \\\\  & =d-c+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
